ke  to be active he's already started to try  to be active on the queenside here and  really he wants to try to find a move to  do here now I'm not really going to talk  about his setup that he has here but I  do want to say a lot of players from  here they don't think that e 2 or D 3 is  very active and so they really want to  get their bishop involved and a lot of  players here I see will bring their  Bishop to g5 because they think that  when they develop their bishop they need  to develop it and pin down this Knight  as you can see here we have a little  problem this Knight here is being  defended by this bishop and the key  problem that we see is our bishop is  undefended  so what black can do hopefully you can  see it here is black can play the move  Knight takes  excuse-me Knight takes on d5  now as you can see here if we take this  bishop on e7 then the knight can come  back and take on e7 and white is down a  pawn and material from that exchange and  there's no real place that white can  play that's going to be good after the  Knight takes on d5 if we take this night  here on d5 with either of our ponds or  even our knight here then the bishops  can go in to come down and take this  Bishop on to five because again this  Bishop on g5 is unprotected so even if  we try Knight takes on d5 then he's  going to come down and take our bishop  on g5 s you can see here black one upon  up the material and that's definitely  not what we want to do in the middle  games we're trying to figure out what  side is going to be aggressive and how  the rest of the game is going to go so  keep in mind here although it's White's  moving you do want to get your bishops  involve your other minor pieces develop  them correctly you always want to make  sure if you're pinning down a knight  that you're pinning it down  to a piece that's not going to end up  losing your material now if we didn't  have this pawn here on d5 then that  wouldn't be so bad so if it's an open  game and you bring your Bishop to g5  that's one thing but if you have in this  case a pawn on d5 or another piece on d5  knowing that this knight can come to d5  and still defend this Bishop on d7 you  definitely don't want to bring your  Bishop to g5 trying to pin down this  knight on f6 let's take a look at our  last example and is here you can see  that black right now is pinning down  White's knight on f3 to the Queen on d1  now white really wants to alleviate this  pin so he's going to bring his Bishop to  e2 as you can see here black originally  did not develop his Bishop to g4 with  this Bishop here on e 2 so he didn't go  against the cardinal rule but keep in  mind even though he was pinning this  knight down the bishop is now on e 2 so  he's going to have to do something and  this is where I see a lot of players  make mistakes later on the game not just  the development move Bishop themselves  but later on they really don't know what  to do once they see the bishop come to  e2 because a lot of players want to  bring their Bishop to g4 but they don't  want to trade off on Knight to f3 and so  they get kind of lost in confusion now  black really needs to keep in mind that  this is the same setup that we talked  about before we have this pawn here on  d4 now that the knight can attack and  still defend this bishop so we really  need to if we're going to move along in  development we really need if we want to  keep our Bishop here on g4 we need to  defend this we could bring our Bishop to  or excuse me our Queen to d7 we could  bring our knight to f6 but we want to  avoid moves like pawn to g6  trying to fee in Quetta our bishop to  the g7 square and then castle on the  Kings side it's a move like this that's  really going to get you in trouble  because now as we talked about before  the  can now come tonight takes on d4 and  we're going to go up in material now  white black has a few options he can  take our pitch upon e2 and we retake  with our knight or if he wants to he can  take with his knight but then again we  take his bishop on g4 again as you can  see is very important that we defended  our Bishop on g4 but instead we played a  move like g6 which was it may have  looked good because you're like okay I'm  gonna fit in cut up my bishop and all  you know then castle on the King side  and I'll be really safe but you really  need to keep in mind that once white  played Bishop to e2 we need to before we  have King safety we need to figure out  how not to lose material so as you can  see here coming back this is going to be  bad for black instead of playing pawn to  g6 as soon as we see this pawn to e2 or  excuse me Bishop to e2 we really need to  have a different game plan so I know  this is minor but hopefully this will  help some of you out as far as when  you're pinning down a knight first win  not to and more importantly if you are  pinning it down just something to think  about if you ever do see this Bishop  come to e2 or it doesn't have to be e2  it can be another square as well but if  you're you're pinning it down and then  this Bishop comes here to make sure that  one either your Bishop is being  protected or to that you don't have a  piece or pawn here on d4 in this  situation obviously the pawn on e5  doesn't really matter because when his  knight takes his knight can't defend  this Bishop on e 2 as well so hopefully  this will help you every little bit and  your chess game that you can improve  will really help you overall so  hopefully all these middlegame  tactics and strategies that I'm going  over some may seem kind of minor to you  but hopefully when you put them all  together in your chess game and you  start to see these things over and over  that it'll really help you improve your  total game so if you haven't already  please subscribe to the channel and I  will see you guys in the next video  thanks for watching
 

